# UAT Indexing Policy (Permanent)

This repo has Production and UAT. UAT is strictly for internal testing.

UAT MUST NEVER be discoverable by search engines or impact the production Office Beacon site.

## When this rule applies

Review this policy **before and during** any work touching:

- UAT or staging website delivery
- Renderer public routing (`apps/renderer`)
- SEO, metadata, `<head>`, or structured data (JSON-LD)
- `robots.txt`, `sitemap.xml`, RSS/Atom feeds
- Canonical URLs, hreflang, or Open Graph tags
- Middleware or response headers (`X-Robots-Tag`, `Cache-Control` on public pages)
- Cloudflare, CDN, or deployment configuration that affects crawling
- Website publishing, cache purge, or revalidation that affects public HTML
- Environment templates (`ENVIRONMENT`, `RENDERER_*`, public URLs)

## Hard requirements

1. **UAT/staging websites must never be indexable** — enforce `noindex`, `nofollow`, and `noarchive`.
2. **UAT indexing protection MUST be gated exclusively by `ENVIRONMENT=staging`.** Never introduce `ENVIRONMENT=uat` or any second UAT indexing gate.
3. **Never generate or proxy a production sitemap for UAT** — `/sitemap.xml` must not expose UAT URLs on staging deployments.
4. **Never expose a UAT sitemap intended for search engines** — return 404 or equivalent; do not list UAT pages/posts.
5. **`robots.txt` on UAT must block all crawlers** — `Disallow: /` and **no `Sitemap:` line**.
6. **Maintain defense in depth** — HTML `<meta robots>`, `X-Robots-Tag` response header (including sitemap/feed 404s), and crawl-blocking `robots.txt` where applicable.
7. **Production indexing must remain enabled** — do not apply global noindex; gate all staging behavior on `ENVIRONMENT=staging` only.
8. **Canonical and URL safety** — UAT must never intentionally expose production canonical URLs to search engines; UAT pages must remain `noindex`/`nofollow` even when editor-configured canonical metadata is present; no sitemap or feed may expose UAT URLs. Content-level canonicals may still appear in HTML — noindex + no sitemap/feeds remain the primary indexing protection unless senior approves a broader change.
9. **Add or update tests** whenever indexing behavior changes (`apps/renderer/src/lib/indexing-policy.ts` and affected routes).
10. **Mention UAT indexing safety** in implementation summaries and PR test plans.
11. **Include UAT indexing checks in PR review** for any renderer, SEO, deployment, or public-routing change — confirm staging behavior before merge.

## PR review checklist (when relevant)

- [ ] `ENVIRONMENT=staging` gating verified — production/local unchanged
- [ ] `/robots.txt` blocks all crawlers on staging; no `Sitemap:` line
- [ ] `/sitemap.xml` and feeds return 404 on staging (no production URL leakage)
- [ ] Staging pages emit `noindex, nofollow, noarchive` + `X-Robots-Tag`
- [ ] JSON-LD suppressed on staging; production structured data intact

## Implementation reference (renderer)

| `ENVIRONMENT` | Expected behavior |
|---------------|-------------------|
| `staging` | Block-all `robots.txt`, no sitemap, no feeds, `noindex/nofollow/noarchive` metadata + `X-Robots-Tag`, suppress JSON-LD |
| `production` | Normal SEO — per-site sitemap/robots proxy, page-level metadata unchanged |
| `local` / `development` | Unchanged from production path unless explicitly testing staging |

Central module: `apps/renderer/src/lib/indexing-policy.ts`

## Development gate

Before shipping any feature, ask:

> Could this accidentally expose UAT to search engines or serve production SEO rules on UAT?

If yes or uncertain: stop, explain the risk, implement the safest environment-aware solution.

## Prohibited without senior approval

**Never remove, weaken, bypass, or disable UAT indexing protection** without explicit senior approval.

This includes: deleting staging checks, falling back to `Allow: /` robots on UAT, re-enabling sitemap/feeds on staging, or applying global noindex that affects production.

This policy overrides convenience until explicitly revoked by the project owner.
